{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2722 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7f67da73a363f385e4c83262c586d56e3f35014a9f41866fa0d3437251fab401" :edb/db-before-t 2721 :edb/db-after-t 2722 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536034 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:19:27.644Z" 13194139536034 true] [17592186163996 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380227 13194139536034 true] [17592186163996 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380227 13194139536034 true] [17592186166440 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074064/assets/075eca4fcc837dce57c4bcfba01047af3d72f15b80cd3fabfc7e5b00be83813d.png)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}.$ \nFind the solution of the new system in terms of the parameter $s.$" 13194139536034 true] [17592186166440 1100 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$.\nFind the solution of the new system in terms of the parameter $s$." 13194139536034 false] [17592186166440 1145 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-3L{x_{1}(t)} + L{x_{2}(t)}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 3L{x_{1}(t)} - L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to the following system:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 4 =-3X_{1}(s) + X_{2}(s); sX_{2}(s) - 4 = 3X_{1}(s) - X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - X_{2}(s) = 4; - 3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)X_{2}(s) = 4\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = (s+3)X_1(s) - 4\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)[(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4] &= 4 \\\\\n- 3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4(s + 1) &= 4 \\\\\n[(s + 1)(s + 3) - 3]X_{1}(s) &= 4 + 4(s + 1) \\\\\ns(s + 4)X_{1}(s) &= 4s + 8 \\\\\n&= X_{1}(s) = (4(s + 2))/(s(s + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $X_2(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{2}(s) &= (s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4 \\\\\n&= (s + 3)((4(s + 2))/(s(s + 4))) - 4 \\\\\n&= (4(s + 3)(s + 2) - 4s(s + 4))/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4(s^{2} + 5s + 6) - 4(s^{2} + 4s))/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4s^{2} + 20s + 24 - 4s^{2} - 16s)/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4(s + 6))/(s(s + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nX_1(s) =\\dfrac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\dfrac{4(s+6)}{s(s+4)}\n$$" 13194139536034 false] [17592186166440 1145 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L\\,{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L\\,{-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ L\\,{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L\\,{3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL\\,{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=-3L\\,{{x}_{1}(t)}+L\\,{{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ sL\\,{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=3L\\,{{x}_{1}(t)}-L\\,{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=-3{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s)-{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-{X}_{2}(s)=4 \\\\ -3{X}_{1}(s)+(s+1){X}_{2}(s)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = (s+3)X_1(s) - 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{X}_{1}(s)+(s+1)\\,[(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-4] & =4 \\\\ -3{X}_{1}(s)+(s+1)(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-4(s+1) & =4 \\\\ [(s+1)(s+3)-3]{X}_{1}(s) & =4+4(s+1) \\\\ s(s+4){X}_{1}(s) & =4s+8 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $X_2(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{2}(s) & =(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-4 \\\\  & =(s+3)\\,(\\frac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)})-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+3)(s+2)-4s(s+4)}{s(s+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({s}^{2}+5s+6)-4({s}^{2}+4s)}{s(s+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{s}^{2}+20s+24-4{s}^{2}-16s}{s(s+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+6)}{s(s+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nX_1(s) =\\dfrac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)}, \n\\qquad\nX_2(s) = \\dfrac{4(s+6)}{s(s+4)}.\n$$" 13194139536034 true] [17592186166441 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074064/assets/243212bee1e50c7ca289142dcc8a9b5ac4f6d06fe3715661e99ec2601c82bf45.png)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ x_1(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{4}{s} + \\dfrac{1}{s-6}, \n\\qquad\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{20}{s} - \\dfrac{1}{s-6}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." 13194139536034 true] [17592186166441 1100 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-5x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{4}{s} + \\dfrac{1}{s-6}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{20}{s} - \\dfrac{1}{s-6}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." 13194139536034 false] [17592186166441 1145 "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{1}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s} + \\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 4L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} + L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 1 + e^{6 \\cdot t} \\\\\n&= 4 + e^{6t} \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{2}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{20}{s} - \\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 20L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} - L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 20 \\cdot 1 - e^{6 \\cdot t} \\\\\n&= 20 - e^{6t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{6t} \\\\ 20 - e^{6t}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{6t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536034 false] [17592186166441 1145 "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{1}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =4{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{2}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{20}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =20{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 1-{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =20-{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ 20-{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536034 true] [17592186166442 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074064/assets/0be5433ffb8166a35fc9084c59116f77b223e25b85f939cb3c279f983939bc1f.png)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}}=-3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ \\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}}=\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXXXXXXX}[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t),$ respectively." 13194139536034 true] [17592186166442 1100 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 9x_{1}(t) + 5x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n${[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math] =-3X_{1}(s); [math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math] = [math]\\phantom{XXXXXXXX}[/math]$\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$, respectively." 13194139536034 false] [17592186166442 1145 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-3x_{1}(t)}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{9x_{1}(t) + 5x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-3L{x_{1}(t)}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 9L{x_{1}(t)} + 5L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 3 =-3X_{1}(s); sX_{2}(s) - 7 = 9X_{1}(s) + 5X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nare the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$, respectively." 13194139536034 false] [17592186166442 1145 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{-3{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{9{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=-3L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=9L{{x}_{1}(t)}+5L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3=-3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-7=9{X}_{1}(s)+5{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t),$ respectively." 13194139536034 true] [17592186166862 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380228 13194139536034 true] [17592186166862 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380244 13194139536034 true] [17592186166862 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380260 13194139536034 true] [17592186166862 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380276 13194139536034 true] [17592186166862 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380292 13194139536034 true] [17592186166863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380308 13194139536034 true] [17592186166863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380313 13194139536034 true] [17592186166863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380318 13194139536034 true] [17592186166863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380324 13194139536034 true] [17592186166864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380330 13194139536034 true] [17592186166864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380339 13194139536034 true] [17592186166864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380349 13194139536034 true] [17592186166864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380358 13194139536034 true] [17592186166864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380367 13194139536034 true] [17592186167101 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536034 true] [17592186167101 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3(2s-5)}{s(s-3)}.$" 13194139536034 true] [17592186167101 1100 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t),x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, the solution of the new system is$X_{1}(s) =$ {{field-1}},\n$X_{2}(s) = (3(2s - 5))/(s(s - 3))$.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-an-initial-value-problem-given-the-laplace-transformed-solution\"></a>" 13194139536034 false] [17592186167101 1145 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}-2L{{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L{{x}_{1}(t)}+2L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-9={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-6=-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(s-1){X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)=9 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)+(s-2){X}_{2}(s)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n